Closing the perf gap in `kernels/fa3_hopper.cu` vs vLLM FA3 baseline on H200 (sm90). Started 2026-07-22.
Target slow scenarios (bench: `python -m bench.eval --set real` + hd256 fp8 configs from attntest).
Baseline geomean over the 12 targets was **0.735x**.

**Iteration is expensive: each `bench.eval` rebuild of the 210KB kernel is ~200s (ptxas over all template instantiations, unavoidable). Batch high-confidence changes; use 2 GPUs (`--gpu 0/1`) + copied .cu variants to test in parallel.** Profile the prebuilt `.so` in `.fa3_trials/<ns>/` with `FA3_NS=<ns> FA3_CFG=<cfg> ncu ... python -m bench.prof_real` (no rebuild).

**SESSION 7 (2026-07-23) — NCU-GROUNDED the two residuals + one real win. CORRECTS the earlier "register-capped / needs CUTLASS register allocator" framing for hd256:**
- **WIN (committed 98416f1): persistent-consumer release-before-pack.** The per-tile prefill_kernel consumer released `empty[pst]` at wgmma_wait0 (before the register-only rescale/pack), but the PERSISTENT consumer (prefill_persist_kernel, used by dense hd128/hd192/hd256) released it LATE (after rescale+pack). Moving it to right after wgmma_wait0 → producer refills ~130cyc sooner. **dense hd256 0.878→0.89**; hd128/hd192/seq16k + all 10 real paged shapes NEUTRAL (paged uses per-tile, already had it). Safe: stage is fully consumed at wgmma_wait0; rescale/pack touch only registers.
- **hd256 (0.89) diagnosis is TENSOR DENSITY, not occupancy/registers/barriers (all NCU-measured, GPU idle):** FA3 (`FlashAttnFwdSm90` tile `<128,80,256>` kStages=2) and our persistent kernel BOTH run **1 CTA/SM at 18.75% occupancy** (168 regs + 213KB smem each cap at 1 block; 384 thr; 12 warps — IDENTICAL), and BOTH stall similarly per issue (barrier ~2.0-2.3, wgmma-wait ~1.3-1.5, long_scoreboard ~1.0). FA3 gets **59% SM-compute vs our 46%** purely because m64n80 QK/PV packs 1.67x work/issue vs our m64n48. FA3's CN=80 fits only at kStages=2 (Q64KB+2*(K+V)160KB=224KB, no Vst in the dense TMA path). **Our STAGES=2 STARVES: measured CN=80/STAGES=2 = 0.75 (correct, swizzle valid!), long_scoreboard 0.94→2.28, barrier 2.0→3.62 — the 2-stage producer can't hide the 80KB load; release-before-empty did NOT fix it (0.746).** STAGES=3 hides the load (long_sb 0.94) but smem caps CN at 48 (56/64 overflow 227KB). tile_size.h line 51 = `{128, is_local?64:80, true, true}`. Both QK (has m64n80 branch) and PV (CN/16=5) already support CN=80. The wall is FA3's CUTLASS async-TMA pipeline hiding the 2-stage load (single wide swizzled box + producer-acquire); our hand-issued HD/64 serial boxes from one producer lane (persist producer load_kv, line ~1585) can't match its prefetch. Parallelizing box-issue across lanes is the ONLY untried lever but risks mbarrier-arrival deadlock (like the cn_eff hang) + STAGES=2 barrier 3.62 also needs fixing → deferred. ping-pong hd256 = neutral (confirmed). CM=64 = 0.72.
- **llama8b_pf_s2k (0.925) / s16k (0.945) diagnosis:** per-tile paged prefill, 48% compute, dominant stall wgmma-wait 1.58 (+ long_sb 0.98, barrier LOW 0.74). FA3's paged-prefill kernel uses a **VarlenDynamicPersistentTileScheduler (persistent!)**, 56% compute, with HIGHER per-issue stalls (wait 1.81, long_sb 1.47) — again denser/better-scheduled. FA3 amortizes the pipeline-fill prologue across a continuous ring (132 CTAs pay it once); our per-tile grid pays it ~8 waves (batch2×seq2048: total_work≈1024 tiles/132 SM). s2k<s16k because short seq → prologue is a bigger fraction. Paged-persist replication is DISABLED (`&& false`, launch_prefill_t ~line 2552) — every prior variant regressed on barrier overhead (1.46 vs FA3's 0.44). This is the one remaining lever for pf but prior-regressed. NOTE: FA3 also launches a 3µs `prepare_varlen_num_blocks_kernel` before each attn kernel.

**SESSION 7c — SPLITKV WORKED. dense hd256 0.89 -> ~0.95 (0.948-0.958). The "floor" conclusion below was WRONG; the separate-K/V pipeline was the missing lever and it's now implemented + committed (1427055).** Ported FA3's separate K/V pipelines into the per-tile prefill_kernel: `full_k/full_v/empty_k/empty_v` barriers (constexpr `SPLITKV = HD==256 && CM==128 && USE_TMA`, so ONLY dense hd256 prefill; decode-split hd256 is CM==64 and would deadlock on full_v since its COOP producer arrives on full[] -- the gate excludes it). Producer issues K->full_k (gated empty_k) then V->full_v (gated empty_v); consumer waits full_k[st] before QK, full_v[pst] before PV, releases empty_k[st] at wgmma_wait1 (QK done, K freed a PV-gemm early) and empty_v[pst] at wgmma_wait0 (PV done). sK0[st]/sV0[pst] are distinct buffers so freeing K mid-PV is safe. Dispatch: hd256 -> per-tile CN=80/STAGES=2 (persistent DISABLED for HD==256; cn_eff=80). Final-PV needs an explicit full_v[last] wait (loop only waits V up to n_cnt-2; also covers n_cnt==1). **NCU proof the mechanism worked: long_scoreboard 2.28->1.17 (load now hidden at 2 stages), barrier 3.62->0.72 (producer stays ahead), compute 40->50%.** Full suite 105/105 correct, no regressions (all hd128/hd192/paged/decode/fp8 unchanged -- SPLITKV compile-gated). **NEGATIVE: adding FA3's warp-scheduler ping-pong on top of SPLITKV REGRESSED hd256 (0.95->0.932) -- the nb_sync/nb_arrive barrier cost exceeds the WG-overlap gain at hd256 (confirms the old "ping-pong neutral" note). Reverted.** hd256 is now ~parity via SPLITKV-only.
- **llama8b_pf_s2k (~0.92) root cause = WAVE-TAIL occupancy loss (NCU-measured SESSION 7c).** Per-tile grid = 1024 blocks / **7.76 waves**, achieved occupancy **16.98% vs 18.75% theoretical** (tail + causal imbalance). FA3's paged kernel is PERSISTENT: grid=132 / **1 wave** / achieved **18.70%** (no tail). Same 168 regs / 1 CTA-SM both. That ~9% relative occupancy loss ≈ the ~8% perf gap. CONSISTENT across seqlen: s16k (batch1/seq16k) = 4096 blocks / 31 waves -> tiny relative tail -> 0.947 (better); s2k (7.76 waves) -> bigger relative tail -> 0.92. **Re-confirmed (7c) the paged-persistent is STILL a dead end even WITH this session's release-early consumer: pf_s2k 0.807, s16k 0.788, qwen 0.817 -- all ~0.80, WORSE than per-tile (0.92/0.95/0.96). The continuous-ring COOP-gather handshake overhead exceeds the tail/prologue savings.** SPLITKV does NOT transfer (pf is neither load- nor barrier-bound: long_sb 0.98, barrier 0.74). Closing pf_s2k needs a persistent kernel whose mainloop is as efficient as the per-tile one -- i.e. FA3's low-overhead static persistent tile scheduler, NOT our dynamic-atomic+bar.sync-1 ring (which regresses to 0.80). That is a scheduler rewrite orthogonal to everything tried. **This is the current architectural limit for short-seq paged prefill; per-tile 0.92 is the best achieved.**
- **(7e) FOUND + FIXED the persistent's MISSING gather optimization, but it still loses to per-tile.** The persistent's load_kv_coop NEVER got the per-tile's offset-broadcast + pow2-divmod (it broadcast the ROW and had all 8 lanes recompute ent*stride = 8x redundant int64 IMADs, + software key/block_size idiv). Porting them (offset-broadcast: compute koff_l/voff_l once, shfl the 64-bit offset; pow2 shift/mask) lifted the paged persistent **0.807->0.876 (pf_s2k), 0.788->0.861 (s16k), 0.817->0.902 (qwen)** -- all 3/3 correct. NCU: long_sb 1.31->1.22, barrier 1.26->1.07, compute 41->46%. **STILL < per-tile (0.925/0.947/0.959) on all three.** The persistent has the occupancy win (18.57 vs 16.98, no wave tail) but a compute deficit (46 vs 48%) from its per-item overhead per-tile lacks: single-buffered Q RELOAD (barQf/barQe handshake -> short_sb 0.60 vs 0.25) + the bar.sync-1 scheduler broadcast (barrier residual). Can't hide the Q reload: QBUF=2 needs PPS=2 (256KB at PPS=3 overflows) and PPS=2 STARVES the COOP gather (0.675, re-confirmed). **So per-tile 0.925 remains the best for pf; the persistent plateaus ~0.876 below it.** The ONE remaining lever = Q-IN-REGISTERS (RS-QK, FA3's Q_in_regs): removes the smem Q buffer + the barQf/barQe handshake, freeing smem for Q double-buffering / deeper ring -- but it's a large change to the SHARED qk_gemm_g (affects the per-tile too) and may not fully close the scheduler residual. Not yet attempted. **The offset-broadcast+pow2-divmod port is a correct persistent improvement (kept in git reflog / this note) but dead code while the persistent stays disabled (per-tile wins).**
- **(7d) IMPLEMENTED + MEASURED the SPLITKV collective rewrite in the PAGED PERSISTENT (separate K/V pipelines in the COOP gather + staggered release, gated SPLITKV_P = !USE_TMA && hd128). It is NUMERICALLY CORRECT (1/1, no deadlock -- validates the split-COOP-gather + continuous-ring + ping-pong mbarrier accounting) but REGRESSED to 0.723 (worse than combined-persistent 0.807 AND per-tile 0.925).** Root cause: pf is NOT load-bound (unlike hd256), so the separate-K/V split buys no load-hiding; instead the single-thread producer's sequential [wait empty_k -> K -> wait empty_v -> V] serializes (the next tile's K blocks behind the current tile's empty_v wait), and the two PF_PUBLISH arrivals add overhead. The SPLITKV mechanism that WON hd256 (2-stage load-hiding) BACKFIRES on pf. Reverted. **DEFINITIVE (measured, not conjectured): every persistent variant for paged pf regresses (combined 0.807, SPLITKV 0.723) below per-tile 0.925; the collective rewrite has now been tried and does not close pf_s2k. Per-tile 0.925 is the architectural limit; the only remaining path is FA3's exact cp.async collective, which I cannot match without reimplementing CUTLASS's async pipeline.**
- **(7c FINAL) pf_s2k is at a MEASURED architectural limit -- both configs profiled, neither reaches 0.95, FA3 wins on BOTH axes:** per-tile occ 16.98% / compute 48% -> 0.925 (wave tail); paged-persistent occ 18.57% (tail FIXED, ~=FA3's 18.70) / compute 41% -> 0.807 (cp.async gather stalls MORE in the continuous ring: long_sb 1.31 vs per-tile 0.98, short_sb 0.66 vs 0.25, barrier 1.26 vs 0.74); FA3 occ 18.70 / compute 56. The persistent trades tail-fix for gather-inefficiency and nets 0.807 (12pp BELOW per-tile). SPLITKV-in-persistent can't close +18% (it gave hd256 only +6pp, and the 0.807 baseline is too low to beat per-tile 0.925). Split-KV-PREFILL is out: total_q=4096*32heads -> Opart ~536MB -> combine ~350us >> 176us kernel (that's why prefill never splits; FA3 doesn't either). **CONCLUSION: per-tile 0.925 is the best; closing the last 2.5pp requires FA3's efficient cp.async-gather-in-persistent collective (occ AND compute), i.e. reimplementing CUTLASS's async pipeline for the paged gather -- no localized fix exists. This is the faithful-cutlass-free limit for short-seq paged prefill.** hd256 (SPLITKV, closed) had a clean localized mechanism; pf_s2k does not.
- **(7c) READ FA3 tile_scheduler.hpp: the SCHEDULER is NOT the pf blocker.** FA3's DynamicPersistentTileScheduler (L284-361) also uses atomicAdd(tile_count_semaphore) + a 2-named-barrier smem broadcast on warp0 -- structurally the SAME as our dynamic-atomic + bar.sync-1. StaticPersistentTileScheduler (L172) is atom-free (tile_idx += gridDim.x) but needs the divmod tile-ordering for balance. DECISIVE evidence the blocker is the cp.async GATHER-in-ring, not scheduling: our DENSE persistent (dynamic sched + TMA) = 1.044 (beats FA3), our PAGED persistent (SAME sched + COOP gather) = 0.80. Only TMA-vs-COOP differs. Also: pf's wave-tail imbalance is intra-wave DURATION variance (causal m-tiles differ in length; a wave finishes at its slowest CTA, idling the light ones) -- fundamental to the wave model, which only a persistent ring removes; grid-reorder can't fix duration variance, and global-LPT already regressed on L2 reuse. So pf_s2k (~0.92) needs an EFFICIENT cp.async-COOP-gather continuous ring (FA3 achieves it; our 4 variants regress to 0.80), a deep systemic producer rewrite -- NOT a localized fix like SPLITKV. This is the current architectural limit; per-tile 0.92 is best.
- **(superseded) llama8b_pf_s2k (0.925) is NOT closed by SPLITKV and won't be:** it is NOT load- or barrier-bound in the per-tile kernel (long_scoreboard 0.98, barrier 0.74) -- SPLITKV's win is 2-stage load-hiding + producer-lead which pf doesn't need. pf's gap is wgmma-wait 1.58 + the per-tile pipeline-fill prologue paid ~8 waves (short seq 2048 -> prologue is a big fraction). FA3 uses a persistent tile scheduler (barrier 0.44). Our paged-persistent is DISABLED (every variant regressed: PPS=2 0.62, PPS=3 0.80, prefetch-sched 0.72 -- all worse than per-tile+LPT 0.925; release-early won't close a 12pp gap). Closing pf needs a LOW-OVERHEAD persistent scheduler (FA3-style static tile scheduler, NOT our dynamic-atomic + bar.sync-1 broadcast whose barrier=1.46), a separate scheduler rewrite. s16k (0.947) + qwen_pf (0.959) are already ~parity, so pf_s2k (shortest seq) is the lone holdout.

**SESSION 7b — READ FA3's mma mainloop (mainloop_fwd_sm90_tma_gmma_ws.hpp) and found the EXACT mechanism our kernel structurally cannot express.** FA3 uses **THREE SEPARATE async pipelines: pipeline_k, pipeline_v, pipeline_vt** (lines 294-300), each with its own producer_acquire/consumer_wait/consumer_release. The consumer `fwd_step` (lines 1244-1281) releases them at DIFFERENT points within one iteration:
  1. `consumer_wait(pipeline_k, read+1)` — wait NEXT K (only WG0; line 1249).
  2. `warp_scheduler_barrier_sync()` — ping-pong wait my WG's issue turn.
  3. QK gemm (next) [wg_wait=-1], PV gemm (current) [wg_wait=-1] — both issued.
  4. `warp_scheduler_barrier_arrive()` — release other WG.
  5. `warpgroup_wait<1>()` (QK done, PV still in flight) → **`pipeline_k.consumer_release`** — K freed HERE, before softmax.
  6. softmax on QK result.
  7. `warpgroup_wait<0>()` (PV done) → **`pipeline_v.consumer_release`** — V freed HERE, ~1 full PV-gemm LATER than K.
**Our kernel keeps K+V in ONE combined stage with ONE `empty[]` release at wgmma_wait0.** We CANNOT release K early (releasing the shared stage while PV still reads V corrupts it). That combined ring is exactly why our STAGES=2 producer can't refill K deep enough to hide the 80KB load (long_scoreboard 2.28), while FA3's independent pipeline_k refills K a full PV-gemm sooner → hides it at 2 stages → CN=80 → 59% compute. **The fix is a core-mainloop rewrite: separate full_k/full_v/empty_k/empty_v barriers + split producer K/V issue + split consumer waits/releases (K before QK, V before PV).** Plus FA3's warp_scheduler_barrier ping-pong is ALWAYS-ON for IntraWGOverlap (UseSchedulerBarrier) and COUPLED with "only WG0 waits K/V" (frees WG1 issue slots) — our hd256 ping-pong (off, measured neutral) was tested WITHOUT the separate pipelines, so its neutrality doesn't rule out the combined mechanism. **Assessment: reaching hd256/pf parity needs the FULL integrated collective (3 separate pipelines + always-on scheduler-barrier ping-pong + persistent tile scheduler), not any single piece — a multi-day CUTLASS-collective port, high deadlock risk, NOT an in-session tuning tweak. Piecemeal replication (release-early ✓ banked +1pp, ping-pong ✗ neutral, box-parallel ✗ issue-not-transfer-latency) each gets partway at best.** This is the concrete, source-grounded floor for the current single-combined-ring architecture.

Key FA3 mechanisms reverse-engineered (reference/vllm-flash-attention/hopper/):
- **Paged decode/prefill (page_size=1) uses cp.async, NOT TMA** (tile_size.h: paged_kv_non_TMA → kBlockN=128). FA3 hits 89% DRAM BW at the SAME 12.5% occupancy / num_splits=1 as us. The lever is CN=128 + a barrier-free page-pointer resolve (each thread loads 1 page_table entry, `__shfl` broadcasts across the 8 threads/row). Its num_splits heuristic returns 1 when batch*heads already fills the SMs.
- **fp8 hd256 uses RS-PV** (P kept in registers, MmaPV_is_RS=true), CN=128, IntraWGOverlap. Not a smem P round-trip.

Changes that WORKED (in fa3_hopper.cu working tree):
1. Decode deep CN 64→128 (`DCN_D` for hd128 in launch_decode_split): decode DRAM 41→53%, geomean of 7 decode cfgs way up. **CN halving the tile count (per-tile softmax/pipeline overhead) is the win, NOT more pipeline stages.**
2. Barrier-free COOP gather (prefill_kernel producer): replace 2-pass smem-prefetch + `bar.sync` with per-lane page LDG + `__shfl`. decode DRAM 53→57%, s16k 0.72→0.81. **MUST spread the page LDGs across all 128 lanes** — concentrating on col_th==0 + shfl was 2.6x SLOWER (reproduced the old "serialized 2x" warning). [[fa3-cuda-eval-harness]]
3. fp8 hd256 → RS-PV at CN=128/STAGES=2 (build_P_rs_fp8/pv_fp8_rs templated on CN; drop sP smem tile; gated `RS=(HD==256)`). [status: in progress]

4. fp8 hd256 decode CN 64→128 (launch_decode_split_fp8 FCN): 0.76→0.89x (mirrors bf16 decode CN win).
5. Persistent paged prefill: added a COOP-gather branch (`if constexpr(!USE_TMA)`) to prefill_persist_kernel + a paged persist launch branch, GATED to total_work<=12*num_sm (helps small-grid s2k/qwen +2-5%, neutral/regresses huge-grid s16k where per-tile HW scheduling is already optimal). Dense TMA persist path untouched.
7. **fp8 COOP coalesced gather (BIG win)**: the fp8 producer (prefill_fp8_kernel issue_load) was still using the NAIVE 1-thread-per-key gather (gather_row_k8_cp/gather_row_plain8_cp) = uncoalesced (32 rows-256B-apart/warp = 32 scattered sectors), pinning fp8 prefill at ~19k long_scoreboard. Replaced with the same coalesced COOP (TPR8=HD/16 lanes/key + __shfl page resolve), gated COOP8 (needs TPR8 power-of-2 dividing WG; hd96 keeps per-key fallback). hd256 fp8 prefill 0.841->0.917, decode 0.887->1.022. THIS was the fp8 lever, not just RS-PV.
8. **bf16 rescale-reschedule (FA3 schedule)**: our qk_rs_pv_g put rescale_o (64 ALU) BETWEEN the QK(i+1) and PV(i) wgmma issues, delaying PV and cutting QK||PV overlap (34% tensor vs FA3 56%). FA3 issues QK+PV back-to-back then rescales O AFTER PV. Added qk_pv_g (no rescale) + moved rescale_o_g to after wgmma_wait0 in both prefill_kernel and prefill_persist_kernel consumer loops; removed the epilogue pre-PV rescale. Online-softmax stays correct (O rescaled to running max at END of each step, ready for next step's PV). [measuring v10]
9. Async page-row ring (cp.async page entries into smem double-buffer, gather reads smem not LDG) — TRIED, REVERTED (s16k 0.73->0.475: per-tile pfull/pempty+fence overhead >> the LDG it hid on many-tile prefill). Diagnosis of page-LDG-on-critical-path was WRONG.

**BIG WIN (v14): COOP gather TPR=8, not 16.** Matching FA3's kGmemThreadsPerRow=8 (8 lanes/row, KIT=HD/64 chunks/lane = 128-byte/1-cache-line bursts) instead of 16 lanes/row (256-byte/2-line bursts) lifted bf16 PAGED prefill 0.69-0.73->0.77-0.84x AND decode long-ctx 0.82->0.91x. The 15.6-sectors/req "optimal coalescing" metric HID this: 256B spans 2 cache lines and the memory system is far more efficient with 128B single-line requests. LESSON: don't reason away access-granularity; TEST it. (prefill_kernel COOP + persist load_kv_coop both TPR=8 now; COOP gate changed to HD%64==0.)

bf16 PAGED PREFILL — earlier lever (v13): FA3 paged-prefill profile (device_kernel, ncu -k "device_kernel") is tensor 55.8% / SFU(xu) 29% at 1 CTA/SM; ours was 34.9% / 19.3% at the SAME occupancy. Cause: our PV emitted N SEPARATE wgmma groups (pv_box64 committed per 64-col box -> QK+PV = 3 groups for hd128), so wgmma_wait1 left only the LAST box in flight to overlap softmax. Fix: pv_boxes now wraps ALL boxes in ONE wgmma group (single fence + all pv_box64_body + single commit) -> the full PV overlaps softmax (FA3's 2-group QK/PV pipeline). Affects all bf16/fp16 prefill+decode. [measuring v13]

bf16 PAGED PREFILL residual (0.69-0.73) — earlier ruled out structural causes: coalescing optimal (15.6 sec/req), NO register-serialization (C7512 warning only on hd192, never hd128), occupancy matched FA3 (1 CTA/SM), rescale placement matched FA3, 4 page-LDG-hiding attempts all regressed. Dense bf16 prefill (TMA, near-free 1-thread producer) BEATS FA3 at 1.03x; paged uses cp.async (128-thread producer, 2048 loads/tile). Both FA3 and we use cp.async for page_size=1; FA3's is 1.45x more efficient => residual is CUTLASS instruction-scheduling quality on the cp.async paged mainloop at 1 CTA/SM, NOT a structural/algorithmic gap. Levers exhausted without a further win.

6. 1-tile page-row prefetch in the COOP gather — TRIED, REVERTED. Made decode/prefill ~1-2% WORSE: the page-LDG was already hidden by the STAGES pipeline; the prefetch only added register pressure. Confirms the prefill bottleneck is NOT the page LDG but deep mainloop scheduling (wgmma-wait + load latency unhidable at 1 CTA/SM), which CUTLASS's generated code overlaps better — a hand-tuning-vs-CUTLASS gap.

